{"authored" [{"question" "q-136337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The velocity of a particle is $(5i-3j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979841/state.json" "file_sha256" "42965e5fc2ea3e089275e8951dae9b31ceda37cfd57684be32934f423deb3ee2" "basis" 1082 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979841/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-136337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=5i-3j,\\,a=i+2j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(5i-3j)+(i+2j)(2) \\\\  & =(5i-3j)+(2i+4j) \\\\  & =(7i+j)\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979841/state.json" "file_sha256" "42965e5fc2ea3e089275e8951dae9b31ceda37cfd57684be32934f423deb3ee2" "basis" 1082 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979841/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-136337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f299e7929890cff147e1e9313cb7800670f9c1cb6cf26837c9cb420b0275ee4" "basis" 1082 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/1